A||N||4||rhauser||E-Brake Anti Rattle Clips||||1||
Z||000000||rhauser||08-09-2001||07:27 PM||||I've had a terrible rattle for a couple of weeks and I figured out that I am missing one of the little clips that hold the E-Brake cable up against the rear of the car when it is disengaged.  Does anyone know the part number for these? I am missing the one towards the center of the car but the other one is fairly rusted as well.<P>This is for an 85 GT <P>Thanks in advance,<BR>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||08-09-2001||08:07 PM||||More likely you are missing or have worn out one of the inner pad retaining clips in the caliper.||||reg||1
Z||000002||btoth||08-09-2001||10:02 PM||||My rear calipers have always rattled.  When I use the e-brake they will stop until I drive around for a bit and then they will start rattling once again.  It's very annoying.  I've them checked out twice from different people and they always say that they look fine.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mark||08-10-2001||12:34 AM||||You really have to take the caliper off to properly inspect the inner pad retaining clip.  The inner pad should "snap" securely into the little fingers of the retaining clip. The clip should also be properly engaged in the groove of the caliper piston.  If the fingers don't hold the pad securely, carefully bend the fingers inward slightly (or replace the clip) as necessary.  If the inner pad is not held securely by the retaining clip, it will rattle between the caliper piston and the rotor surface and make quite a lot of noise!||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||08-10-2001||07:32 PM||||it will also wear unevenly and faster than it should.||||reg||1
